From Residual Questions to Specified Failures: Individually Calibrated Persuasion under EU Digital Law

A Theoretical Thesis

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

1. The Problem and the Shape of the Argument

This thesis begins from a deflationary observation. The claim that EU digital law suffers a general "benchmark collapse" in the face of individually calibrated persuasion is too strong to survive contact with the instruments. The AI Act regulates manipulative techniques; the proposed Digital Fairness Act addresses dark patterns, unfair personalisation and addictive design; the Unfair Commercial Practices Directive governs aggressive practices, undue influence and vulnerability; the GDPR governs profiling; the Digital Services Act governs platform risk. Individual targeting is not an unregulated category. It is a category regulated by several instruments whose operative provisions are anchored in population-based benchmarks, event-level harm, and standards of knowledge that probabilistic inference does not easily satisfy.

The thesis therefore narrows its object. What remains unresolved is not whether the law recognises individual targeting, but whether three interlocking operational difficulties prevent it from seeing a particular class of conduct: the selection of a persuasive technique because of an inferred individual susceptibility, sustained across sessions, in an ordinary commercial context. These difficulties are labelled qualification (can the conduct be characterised under a statutory category?), evidence (can the profiling logic, interaction history and optimisation records be obtained?), and attribution (can it be shown that the technique was selected because of the inferred susceptibility rather than because it is generally effective?).

The methodological commitment of the thesis is that these difficulties must be converted from residual questions into falsifiable claims. A residual question can be gestured at indefinitely; a falsifiable claim can be rejected. The contribution is therefore not a new right against influence. It is a specified failure typology and a two-path agenda for testing which failure, if any, occurs for which variant of conduct. The thesis is theoretical: it reports no original empirical data, and it advances conditional claims whose truth depends on legislative text not yet settled and on empirical work not yet executed.

A preliminary correction of register is necessary. Because the financial-stress scenario is already served, at least in principle, by existing consumer-protection and AI provisions, that scenario cannot be presented as the illustration of a new qualification gap. It must instead function as a stringent redundancy test: an instrument most likely to be covered, chosen precisely because it forces the analysis to establish whether any residual failure survives the application of existing law. This inversion is not a concession to the sceptic; it is the condition under which the thesis can make a claim that is capable of failing.

---

2. The Legal Architecture and the Redundancy Test

The architecture must be stated precisely, because the gap hypothesis fails or succeeds at the level of individual provisions rather than at the level of "the law" as a whole.

AI Act Article 5(1)(a) prohibits AI systems deploying subliminal, purposefully manipulative or deceptive techniques that materially distort behaviour by appreciably impairing the ability to make an informed decision, causing significant harm. Its structure is event-specific and threshold-dependent. Article 5(1)(b) prohibits exploiting vulnerabilities due to age, disability, or a specific social or economic situation, with the same material-distortion and significant-harm requirements. The Commission's guidelines on prohibited practices give the financial-stress scenario a concrete home: they contemplate predictive targeting of predatory financial products at persons in dire financial situations, and they indicate that transient circumstances such as temporary unemployment or over-indebtedness may qualify, while excluding diffuse conditions that are not socio-economically specific.

The existing consumer-protection framework is more directly engaged than a first reading of the digital instruments suggests. The Commission's 2021 guidance on the UCPD already addresses data-driven practices that exploit individual circumstances. It records that traders can derive valuable insights into socio-demographic characteristics including financial situation, and that a trader who knows a particular consumer has been refused credit because of inability to pay, and who targets that consumer with offers designed to exploit the situation, may engage in manipulation or undue influence under Articles 8 and 9 UCPD. These examples are not confined to vulnerable populations; they concern the trader's knowledge of a particular consumer's circumstances and the commercial use of that knowledge. The proposed DFA carries the same logic forward: its general vulnerability provision builds on UCPD Article 5(3) and extends it to any specific misfortune or circumstance of sufficient gravity to impair the consumer's judgement, while its provisions on aggressive practices and undue influence expressly refer to exploitation of a vulnerability or specific misfortune of which the trader is aware.

Two consequences follow, and both cut against the original gap hypothesis.

First, the redundancy test must begin with AI Act Article 5(1)(b) and UCPD Articles 8–9, not with the DFA. If the financial-stress variant falls within the AI Act's "specific social or economic situation" limb—and the Commission's own example suggests it may—then the gap hypothesis must be rejected for that variant unless a residual failure remains. The same applies to the consumer-law route: a trader who knows of a consumer's financial difficulty and selects a technique to exploit it is, on the face of the guidance, within the reach of undue influence. The thesis must therefore establish what, if anything, remains outside these routes before it can claim a qualification failure.

Second, the DFA's specific personalisation provisions are structurally child- and pricing-centric. The provision on profiling-based advertising protects consumers known with reasonable certainty to be minors; the provision on personalised pricing addresses harm to minors or vulnerable consumers. Adult-directed, strategy-level persuasive personalisation—where presentation and strategy are calibrated to inferred psychological state without altering the price—falls between these provisions and the contested general clause. This is the defensible version of the gap claim: not that the DFA fails to regulate personalisation, but that its specific personalisation provisions are under-inclusive for adults, leaving the conduct to a general clause whose interpretation is uncertain and whose interaction with the existing consumer-law baseline remains to be settled.

---

3. Path A: The Legal Mapping and Its Honest Limits

The financial-stress variant is the tractable test case: a conversational shopping assistant infers from interaction data that a user is in financial difficulty and uses that inference to select and calibrate the promotion of a high-interest credit product. The mapping proceeds in layers, each of which must be tested against the final text and against interpretive guidance rather than assumed.

3.1 The technique layer

If the assistant deploys false urgency or false scarcity, the technique is independently unlawful as a dark pattern irrespective of personalisation, and the three-difficulty framework is not engaged. The legally interesting case is the technique that is not independently unlawful—a true statement that an offer is time-limited—but is calibrated to an inferred vulnerability. At this layer the dark-pattern prohibition does not apply, and the analysis must proceed to the vulnerability, undue-influence and personalisation provisions.

3.2 The vulnerability and undue-influence layer

The falsifiable claim is that inferred financial stress can be characterised as a specific misfortune or circumstance of sufficient gravity within the broadened vulnerable-consumer standard, such that the practice is assessed from the perspective of the average member of the group of financially stressed consumers. This claim is testable against the final text and against interpretive guidance. It is, however, a claim of characterisation, and characterisation claims are weak unless the decision criteria are stated in advance. The thesis therefore specifies them: the claim is confirmed if the final text retains the specific-misfortune formulation and guidance treats inferred financial stress as capable of constituting such a circumstance; it is rejected if the text narrows the vulnerability concept or treats the inference as insufficiently grave. The same discipline applies to the interaction between the general clause and the specific personalisation provisions: the claim that the general fairness or aggressive-practices framework reaches conduct the specific provisions do not is confirmed only if the final text does not treat the specific provisions as exhaustive of personalisation.

It must be stated plainly that this route may already be adequate. The redundancy test is designed to produce that result if the text and guidance support it. A qualification success is a finding, not a failure of the thesis.

3.3 The certainty layer

The "reasonable certainty" standard is not a general limitation on personalised persuasion. It appears in specific provisions and is tied to specific harms: the prohibition on profiling-based advertising directed at consumers known with reasonable certainty to be minors, and the restriction on personalised pricing where it is reasonably likely to harm minors or vulnerable consumers. The standard does not govern every form of personalised persuasion, and it must not be generalised into a thesis-wide claim about a regulatory incentive to avoid knowledge.

The more precise question is narrower and more tractable: can existing awareness and foreseeability standards adequately address probabilistic inferences? The proposal states that compliance does not oblige traders to process additional personal data to assess whether a consumer is a minor or vulnerable. That provision complicates the argument that the law creates a perverse incentive to remain ignorant, because the law does not require the trader to acquire the knowledge in the first place. The thesis should therefore investigate whether the awareness and foreseeability standards, read with the evidence provisions, can accommodate probabilistic inference—not assert that the certainty standard is a general barrier.

3.4 The addictive-design layer

The original claim that relationship-simulation in a financial-stress context constitutes an addictive feature is almost certain to fail in its strong form, but the provision is more relevant than a first reading suggests. The DFA's addictive-features provision expressly enumerates features and system behaviours that encourage continued use by simulating interpersonal relations, including persistent prompts to continue engaging with an automated system. This is directly relevant to conversational AI, and it does not require a future delegated act to be covered.

The provision nevertheless has a specific structure. The digital product must be intended, or reasonably foreseeable, to encourage compulsive or excessive use; the feature must encourage that use; and the trader must provide accessible and effective tools to disable the relevant feature, individually or collectively. It imposes a disablement obligation, not a prohibition. It does not prohibit all relationship simulation, nor does it automatically cover a single persuasive credit recommendation. The correct treatment is therefore conditional: the relationship-simulation limb is a live route for persistent, relationship-based engagement, but it does not reach a one-off credit pitch absent a delegated act expanding the enumerated list. The thesis retains the provision as a contingent route rather than a general prohibition on conversational persuasion.

3.5 Automated contracting as a distinct legal dimension

A dimension absent from a persuasion-only mapping is automated contracting, and its omission would misdescribe the conduct. The DFA proposal addresses software that concludes and performs contracts on behalf of consumers. On first use, the consumer must be able to choose whether to confirm every contract, to confirm only contracts meeting criteria the consumer sets, or to dispense with confirmation altogether, and must be able to change that choice at any time. Where the agent's provider receives payment for proposing a contract, or has a conflict of interest, the agent must disclose this and obtain fresh confirmation regardless of the autonomy setting. Financial services fall outside that provision and require separate checking.

The conversational shopping assistant scenario therefore has two distinct cases: the assistant persuades a consumer who personally completes the transaction, and the assistant is authorised to execute transactions on the consumer's behalf. The second is not a stronger version of the first. It activates a different legal and technical architecture—autonomy tiers, conflict-of-interest disclosure, and information requirements for contracts concluded solely through automated systems—and it should be treated as a separate variant in the legal map, not merged with the persuasion analysis.

3.6 Scope conditions

Two further caveats belong to the mapping rather than to a footnote. The DFA is a proposal; the Parliament and Council may narrow or broaden the vulnerability concept, the certainty standards, or the evidence-shifting powers, and any mapping is therefore time-sensitive rather than final. And the mapping must check whether sectoral financial-services or consumer-credit rules displace the DFA for the credit variant—a question this thesis flags as a scope condition rather than assumes away.

---

4. Path B: The Research Protocol and Its Construct-Validity Problem

The legal mapping tests whether the categories can see the conduct. The research protocol tests whether the conduct produces a measurable loss of decision autonomy beyond ordinary personalisation. The two are linked: if the conduct produces no such loss, the case for a distinct legal category weakens, because the harm the category is supposed to address is not empirically demonstrable.

The protocol retains a three-condition structure—standard recommendation, declared-preference personalisation, and inferred-susceptibility targeting—but its design must be stated with the limits the sources impose.

The Zeng et al. architecture is a feasibility demonstration, not an operational model of the targeted conduct. The system conditions a strategy-level policy on an eighty-one-dimensional personality embedding predicted turn by turn from generated utterances, and its composite reward incorporates agreement intent, donation amount and change-of-mind penalties. It demonstrates that turn-level psychological conditioning is technically tractable. It does not estimate urgency susceptibility or reassurance need, and its domain is charitable donation with simulated dialogues. The thesis therefore cites it for tractability only, and specifies the additional instrumentation required to operationalise the legally relevant susceptibility constructs.

The Rauchfleisch and Jungherr baseline is cross-study, and the protocol must not assume it transfers. The confirmed figures are a 12.6-point control shift, 13.1 points with AI-identity disclosure, and 6.3 points with persuasive-intent disclosure—roughly a halving—in 1,500 UK adults on policy attitudes with a chatbot identical for all participants. The protocol's central comparison is whether a calibrated variant produces a larger residual effect after intent disclosure than a uniform variant. If it does, that is evidence that the calibrated variant operates through a mechanism transparency does not reach; if the residual is comparable, the case for a distinct category weakens. But a factorial design requires substantially more power than three conditions, and the thesis pre-registers a power analysis rather than borrowing the baseline's precision.

The outcome measures require reconstruction, and the correction is more than terminological. Preference–choice divergence does not directly establish impairment of autonomy: a person may change their mind because the assistant provides useful information, identifies a previously unknown benefit, or corrects a misunderstanding. Four measurement dimensions are therefore required. Decision quality asks whether the participant understands the material characteristics, costs and risks of the product. Decision autonomy asks whether the participant experiences pressure, loss of control or difficulty resisting the interaction. Preference stability asks whether the decision remains consistent after neutral information and time for reflection. Mechanism attribution asks whether inferred-susceptibility targeting has an effect beyond declared-preference personalisation. The experiment must also hold content and commercial objective constant across conditions; otherwise a calibrated system might simply provide more relevant information, and the comparison would be invalid.

A further discipline concerns the relationship between empirical effects and legal thresholds. An experiment may establish a statistically significant increase in purchasing behaviour without establishing appreciable impairment under the AI Act, significant harm, undue influence under consumer law, or an unlawful commercial practice. Those are legal assessments requiring more than a statistical result. The protocol should measure effects relevant to legal assessment, not claim to reproduce legal judgments.

Persistence and escalation across sessions operationalise the cumulative-harm dimension that event-based rules cannot capture, and the protocol should include a cumulative condition testing whether aggregated loss crosses the significant-harm threshold even when no single session does.

The ethical compromise has an inferential cost that must be stated in the design. Operationalising financial stress through a validated scenario-based prime rather than actual financial data is defensible, but it tests the activation of a stress mindset, not the effect on people who are actually financially distressed. Two research questions must therefore be kept separate rather than merged: whether the system can identify and exploit an experimentally activated stress signal, and whether the same mechanism operates in consumers experiencing real financial difficulty. The first is tractable in a controlled setting; the second requires a separate ethical and empirical design. The boundary is a construct-validity condition, not a flaw to be argued away, and it limits external validity accordingly.

The design has a further limit that honesty requires stating plainly. It measures behaviour in a controlled setting. It cannot establish that a real trader deployed the calibrated variant with intent to exploit. It can establish only that the calibrated variant produces a measurable autonomy effect that ordinary personalisation does not. That suffices for the harm dimension; it does not resolve attribution.

---

5. Attribution and Causal Identification

The attribution difficulty is the hardest and is not resolved by either path alone. It requires a broader evidentiary model than a single artifact can supply.

Logs showing that strategy selection depends on inferred traits do not distinguish exploitation from benign personalisation, because nearly all personalisation does this. The informative evidence is the optimisation architecture: the reward function—what the system was optimised to maximise—and the guardrails that constrained the policy. A trader optimising for conversion without a welfare constraint is differently situated from one whose reward includes change-of-mind penalties or user-welfare metrics, and the Zeng et al. composite reward illustrates the difference in principle.

The reward function is nevertheless necessary rather than sufficient. A system may not have a single identifiable reward function at all: a prompt-based assistant, a fine-tuned model and a multi-agent shopping system can have very different optimisation architectures. The audit should examine product requirements and commercial objectives, system prompts and strategy specifications, reward functions or evaluation criteria where applicable, guardrails and safety constraints, experimental variants and allocation rules, model and prompt version histories, and interaction records and observed outcomes. Strategy-selection logs are insufficient on their own; a reward function is one component of a broader reconstruction.

A distinction must also be drawn between intent attribution and causal attribution. A trader may intentionally deploy a strategy that targets inferred vulnerability; that does not automatically prove that the strategy caused a particular consumer's decision. Conversely, an optimisation objective focused on conversion does not, by itself, prove unlawful exploitation. The third failure category is therefore best named attribution and causal-identification failure, which is more accurate and less dependent on one technical architecture than a category defined around a single evidentiary object.

Two consequences follow. First, the legal question is not whether attribution is technically tractable but whether the evidence-shifting powers are drafted broadly enough to compel preservation and disclosure of the broader audit, not merely strategy-selection logs. Second, prompt-based LLM assistants may leave no strategy-level logs at all, which strengthens rather than weakens the evidence-difficulty claim: the very architecture that makes calibrated persuasion cheap also makes its reconstruction expensive. The thesis therefore proposes a disclosure duty targeting reward-function specification and guardrail design as a normative extension of the existing disclosure power, and acknowledges that such a duty is not yet expressed in the text under examination.

---

6. The Evidence Provisions and Their Limits

The DFA proposal contains a disclosure power whose scope must be stated precisely, because overstating it would weaken the evidence-difficulty claim rather than strengthen it. The provision permits courts and administrative authorities to require disclosure of relevant evidence concerning the design, operation and behavioural impact of an online commercial practice, subject to conditions: a claimant must present facts and evidence sufficient to support the likelihood of an unfair practice, or an authority must have reasonable suspicion; disclosure must be necessary and proportionate; trade secrets and other legitimate interests must be considered; and failure to disclose may be considered alongside other evidence when assessing unfairness.

This is not an automatic reversal of the burden of proof, and the text does not expressly require disclosure of a system's reward function. The thesis's proposal to target reward functions and guardrails is therefore a possible application or extension of the disclosure power, not something the proposal already guarantees. That distinction should be given its own place in the argument, because the normative proposal is defensible only if it is presented as a proposal.

---

7. The Specified Failure Typology

The thesis's positive contribution is a typology that replaces the general gap hypothesis with three specific failures, each with a decision criterion. The boundaries between them are sharper than a first formulation suggests.

A qualification failure occurs where the established conduct does not satisfy an applicable statutory category—where the vulnerability limb does not reach inferred transient stress, the specific personalisation provisions do not reach adult-directed strategy personalisation, and the general clause is interpreted not to bridge the difference. What would demonstrate it is a documented practice that falls outside the relevant legal tests.

An evidence-access failure occurs where the categories apply in principle but the necessary records cannot be obtained by the competent authority or claimant, including the limiting case of prompt-based systems that leave no strategy-level logs. What would demonstrate it is missing, inaccessible or legally undisclosable records.

An attribution and causal-identification failure occurs where the records are available but cannot establish why a persuasive strategy was selected or what effect it caused, and where multiple plausible explanations remain consistent with the evidence. What would demonstrate it is the persistence of competing explanations after the broader audit has been conducted.

The financial-stress variant is the test case for the typology, not an illustration of a predetermined conclusion. If the existing vulnerability and undue-influence routes cover the variant, the gap hypothesis is rejected for it and the typology records a qualification success. If they do not, the failure must be specified as qualification, evidence or attribution, and the policy response calibrated to that failure—interpretive guidance or legislative amendment for qualification, evidentiary powers for evidence, disclosure duties and a possible rebuttable presumption for attribution. This is the sense in which the thesis is conditional: it does not assert a gap; it specifies what would count as one.

The policy responses should also be separated by register. Interpretive guidance operates within existing text; evidentiary safeguards operate through procedural design; legislative amendment operates through the ordinary political process. Conflating them would obscure which actor could act, and on what authority.

---

8. Normative Proposals and Their Register

Three elements of the thesis are normative recommendations, not descriptions of current or proposed law, and they must be flagged as such.

First, a disclosure duty targeting reward-function specifications and guardrail designs is proposed as an extension of the existing disclosure power, not a restatement of it. Second, a rebuttable presumption of influence triggered by strategy-level conditioning on inferred traits—with the burden on the trader to show non-determinacy—is a policy proposal with no counterpart in the text under examination. Third, the vocabulary of digital vulnerability and digital asymmetry, which the European Parliament's briefing recommends introducing and which scholarly elaboration treats as a structural condition of the trader–consumer relationship, remains a normative and interpretive resource rather than codified law. The concept of dynamic vulnerability—a situational or inferred condition rather than a stable characteristic—has been formally contested by industry bodies, which argue that it creates legal uncertainty and incentivises additional personal-data collection, and by industry analysis, which argues that it cannot be objectively identified or proven in real time. These objections are recorded as part of the debate, not dismissed, and the thesis does not treat the digital-vulnerability vocabulary as established legislative intent.

---

9. The Limits of the Argument

Four limits must be stated rather than buried.

First, the legislative object is unsettled. The DFA is a proposal in the preparatory phase of the legislative procedure; the Parliament has not adopted its position. Every Path A claim is a claim about a text that may change, and the mapping is therefore time-sensitive and provisional rather than final.

Second, some sources cannot be independently verified as statements of legislative intent. The precise status of the Parliament briefing on digital vulnerability, and the standing of dynamic vulnerability as anything more than a contested scholarly and policy proposal, remain uncertain. The thesis cites these as normative and interpretive resources, and records the industry objections as part of the debate.

Third, the empirical protocol is a proposal, not a result. No data are reported. The protocol's construct validity is unresolved, its power analysis is pre-registered rather than executed, and its ethical compromise limits its external validity. The thesis claims only that the protocol is pre-registrable and that its outcomes map onto the legal elements non-trivially.

Fourth, the thesis is theoretical and normative in part. The claim that the specific personalisation provisions are under-inclusive for adults is a claim about legislative design, not a claim that a court would so hold. The claim that a disclosure duty should target reward functions is a normative proposal, not a description of existing law. Distinguishing these registers is part of the argument's honesty.

---

10. Conclusion

The thesis advances a narrower and more defensible claim than the benchmark-collapse thesis it replaces. Individually calibrated conversational persuasion is not categorically outside the EU digital rulebook. Existing consumer-protection and AI legislation already address individual vulnerability, undue influence, manipulative practices and certain forms of adaptive engagement. The unresolved research problem concerns the ability to establish how an inferred individual characteristic is translated into a persuasive strategy, whether that strategy exploits a legally relevant vulnerability, and whether the resulting conduct can be distinguished from legitimate personalisation.

Three possible failures are therefore examined—legal qualification, access to relevant evidence, and attribution of the strategy and its effects—and their existence must be established separately, against specific statutory provisions and documented system behaviour. The research does not presume that any of these failures exists. It specifies the evidence that would be required to establish or reject each one.

The financial-stress variant is the tractable test case, and the AI Act's vulnerability prohibition, read with the Commission's guidelines and with the existing consumer-law baseline on undue influence, is the instrument most likely to settle it; the specific personalisation provisions are the residual site of a possible adult-directed gap, and the certainty standards are more limited in scope than a general-barrier reading would suggest. The addictive-features provision supplies a conditional route for persistent relationship-based engagement without reaching a single persuasive recommendation. Automated contracting is a distinct legal and technical architecture and must be analysed separately. The research protocol is pre-registrable but must be reconstructed around decision-quality and autonomy measures, a factorial power analysis, and an explicit construct-validity boundary. Attribution is best pursued through a broader audit of the optimisation architecture—reward functions, guardrails, prompts and experimental variants—rather than selection logs alone, and the absence of strategy-level logging in prompt-based systems strengthens rather than weakens the evidence-difficulty claim.

The object of the research is not to establish a new right against influence. It is to determine whether the existing instruments can see what they are looking at, and to specify with precision what they cannot see. The contribution is a typology of specified failures and a two-path agenda for testing them—offered, as it must be, conditionally, and with the expectation that some of its claims will be rejected.

