Appendix A: Legal Provisions Mapping — The Digital Fairness Act (COM(2026) 1100) and the Existing Consumer-Protection Baseline

This appendix records the operative textual anchors in the proposed Digital Fairness Act and in the existing consumer-protection framework that the proposal builds upon, distinguishing confirmed provisions from design features that remain contingent on the final legislative text.

A.1 Institutional and Regulatory Form

The DFA was formally presented by the Commission on 11 November 2026 as COM(2026) 1100 final. It takes the form of a directly applicable Regulation, replacing the Unfair Commercial Practices Directive in its entirety and creating a single EU rulebook for business-to-consumer commercial practices, online and offline. The accompanying overhaul of the Consumer Protection Cooperation Regulation would grant the Commission direct enforcement powers, including fines of up to 6 per cent of worldwide annual turnover for widespread infringements with a Union dimension. The proposal is in the preparatory phase of the legislative procedure; the Parliament has not adopted its position.

A.2 Article 10 — Dark Patterns

Article 10 prohibits designing, organising or operating online interfaces in ways likely to deceive, manipulate, or otherwise materially distort consumers' economic behaviour. Specific dark patterns are prohibited outright, with the precise contents of the blacklist subject to a delegated act and future expansion by the Commission.

A.3 Articles 12–13 — Unfair Personalisation

Article 12 restricts profiling-based advertising directed at consumers known with reasonable certainty to be minors. Article 13 restricts personalised pricing based on profiling where it is reasonably likely to cause harm to minors or vulnerable consumers. Crucially, the provision is framed around pricing and minors, not around adult-directed persuasive strategy selection.

A textual detail of significance for the evidence analysis: the proposal states that compliance with these provisions does not oblige traders to process additional personal data to assess whether a consumer is a minor or vulnerable. This limits the inference that the certainty standard creates a perverse incentive to remain ignorant, because the law does not require the trader to acquire the relevant knowledge in the first place.

A.4 Article 17 — Addictive Design

Article 17 requires traders to provide accessible and effective tools allowing consumers to disable addictive features individually or collectively. The enumerated features include autoplay, endless feeds without effective breaks, notifications unrelated to direct user activity, features that encourage frequent engagement through penalties or loss of benefits, and features and system behaviours that encourage continued use by simulating interpersonal relations, including persistent prompts to continue engaging with an automated system. The Commission may update the list through delegated acts. The provision requires a disablement tool, not a prohibition of the feature itself.

The relationship-simulation limb is therefore already listed and does not require a future delegated act to be covered. It remains a conditional route for persistent, relationship-based engagement, but it does not reach a one-off credit pitch absent an expansion of the enumerated list.

A.5 Article 26 — Automated Contracting

Article 26 governs software that concludes contracts on a consumer's behalf. On first use, the consumer must be able to choose whether to confirm every contract, to confirm only contracts meeting criteria the consumer sets, or to dispense with confirmation altogether, and must be able to change that choice at any time. Where the agent's provider receives payment for proposing a contract or has a conflict of interest, the agent must disclose this and obtain fresh confirmation regardless of the autonomy setting. Financial services fall outside Article 26 and require separate checking.

This provision activates a distinct legal and technical architecture from persuasion. The conversational assistant scenario therefore has two cases—persuasion followed by personal completion, and authorised execution—and the second should be treated as a separate variant rather than a stronger version of the first.

A.6 The General Vulnerability Clause and the "Circumstance of Such Gravity" Formulation

The DFA builds on UCPD Article 5(3), extending it to cover any specific misfortune or circumstance of such gravity as to impair the consumer's judgement. The structure of the instrument is such that many new prohibitions target only minors or vulnerable consumers, but outside those groups, the general fairness clause still applies. Whether inferred financial stress qualifies as a circumstance of such gravity remains a question of interpretation against the final text.

A.7 Article 34 — Evidence-Shifting Powers

Article 34 permits courts and administrative authorities to require disclosure of relevant evidence concerning the design, operation and behavioural impact of an online commercial practice, subject to conditions: a claimant must present facts and evidence sufficient to support the likelihood of an unfair practice, or an authority must have reasonable suspicion; disclosure must be necessary and proportionate; trade secrets and other legitimate interests must be considered; and failure to disclose may be considered alongside other evidence when assessing unfairness.

This is not an automatic reversal of the burden of proof, and the text does not expressly require disclosure of a system's reward function. The thesis's proposal to target reward functions and guardrails is therefore a possible application or extension of the disclosure power, not something the proposal already guarantees.

