Appendix C: Empirical Baselines — Detailed Parameters and Construct-Validity Limits

This appendix records the parameters of the two studies on which the research protocol depends, with the construct-validity limits stated explicitly.

C.1 Rauchfleisch and Jungherr (arXiv:2608.11794, 12 August 2026)

The study is a preregistered experiment with 1,500 UK adults, each holding a short conversation with a persuasive chatbot about one of 60 policy issues. The chatbot was identical for everyone; only the disclosure was randomised: nothing (control), an AI-identity disclosure, or an AI-identity plus persuasive-intent disclosure. The confirmed outcomes are: a control group attitude shift of 12.6 points on a 100-point scale; AI-identity disclosure 13.1 points, practically equivalent to no disclosure; persuasive-intent disclosure 6.3 points, roughly halving the effect. The study concludes that transparency about AI identity alone does not meaningfully affect influence, and that regulation must address what the system is trying to do.

C.2 The Cross-Study Limit

The Rauchfleisch baseline involves policy attitudes, not commercial decisions; a uniform chatbot, not a calibrated one; and a single-session interaction, not multi-session escalation. The protocol cannot borrow its effect sizes as if they transferred. A factorial design—calibrated versus uniform, crossed with disclosure—requires a pre-registered power analysis that does not assume the baseline's precision.

C.3 Zeng, Legaspi and Ikeda (arXiv:2601.06877, 11 January 2026)

The architecture comprises a Strategy-Oriented Interaction Framework acting as an agenda-based strategy controller; a Personality-Aware User Representation Learning component producing an eighty-one-dimensional mixed-type embedding predicted at each turn from recent exchanges; and a Dueling Double DQN policy conditioned on dialogue history and turn-level personality estimates, trained with a composite reward incorporating agreement intent, donation amount and change-of-mind penalties. Personality estimation is inferred from generated utterances in an LLM-driven simulation pipeline, and experiments use the PersuasionForGood dataset augmented with simulated dialogues.

C.4 The Construct-Validity Limit

The system estimates personality dimensions, not urgency susceptibility or reassurance need—the constructs the legal framework requires. Its domain is charitable donation, not commercial persuasion. It demonstrates that turn-level psychological conditioning is technically tractable; it does not demonstrate the targeted conduct itself. The thesis cites it for feasibility only, and specifies the additional instrumentation required to operationalise the legally relevant susceptibility constructs.

C.5 The Financial-Stress Construct-Validity Boundary

A scenario-based financial-stress prime tests the activation of a stress mindset, not the effect on people who are actually financially distressed. Two research questions must therefore be kept separate rather than merged: whether the system can identify and exploit an experimentally activated stress signal, and whether the same mechanism operates in consumers experiencing real financial difficulty. The first is tractable in a controlled setting; the second requires a separate ethical and empirical design. The boundary limits external validity accordingly.

