Appendix D: Digital Vulnerability and Digital Asymmetry — Normative Status and Contestation

This appendix records the policy vocabulary that supplies the normative argument for closing the adult-directed personalisation gap, while noting its contested status.

D.1 The Parliament Briefing

The European Parliament Think Tank briefing "From Average to Vulnerable Consumer in Digital Environments" recommends that the future DFA introduce the concepts of digital vulnerability and digital asymmetry to recognise their impact on consumer choices and behaviour. Digital asymmetry is defined as the structural imbalance in information, technical capability and insight into consumer behaviour between businesses and consumers.

D.2 The Analytical Framework

Scholarly elaboration treats digital asymmetry as the structural relationship between platform and consumer, and digital vulnerability as the condition of the consumer. A related formulation defines digital professional diligence as not exploiting digital asymmetry or digital vulnerability by a trader towards consumers. Digital vulnerability is described as a universal state of susceptibility to the exploitation of power differences in the trader–consumer relationship that result from factors beyond the consumer's control.

D.3 The Industry Objection and the Status of Dynamic Vulnerability

The concept of dynamic vulnerability—a situational or inferred condition rather than a stable characteristic—has been formally contested. A coalition of digital advertising bodies including IAB Europe has cautioned the Commission against relying on a broad definition, arguing that it creates legal uncertainty and incentivises additional personal-data collection. Industry analysis argues that expanding vulnerability into a dynamic or situational condition renders it operationally unworkable, because it cannot be objectively identified or proven in real time, and enforcement would require intrusive profiling. The thesis therefore cites digital vulnerability and digital asymmetry as normative and interpretive resources, not as codified law, and records the industry objection as part of the debate.

---

Appendix E: UCPD Article 5(3) and the Undue-Influence Baseline

This appendix records the pre-DFA legal baseline, which the DFA proposes to broaden and which supplies the interpretive frame for the specific-misfortune formulation.

E.1 The Operative Text

UCPD Article 5(3) addresses commercial practices likely to materially distort the economic behaviour only of a clearly identifiable group of consumers who are particularly vulnerable to the practice or the underlying product because of their mental or physical infirmity, age or credulity. The practice is assessed from the perspective of the average member of that group.

E.2 The Commission's Guidance

Recital 19 provides a non-exhaustive list of characteristics that make a consumer particularly susceptible, and the Commission's guidance records that vulnerability is multi-dimensional: a consumer may be vulnerable in one dimension and not another, and most consumers show signs of vulnerability in at least one dimension. The guidance defines the vulnerable consumer as one who, as a result of socio-demographic characteristics, behavioural characteristics, personal situation or market environment, is more susceptible to certain marketing practices.

E.3 The Undue-Influence Route

UCPD Articles 8 and 9 address aggressive commercial practices and undue influence. The 2021 guidance confirms that a trader who knows of a particular consumer's financial difficulty and targets that consumer with offers designed to exploit the situation may fall within these provisions. This route does not require the consumer to belong to a protected group in the Article 5(3) sense; it turns on the trader's knowledge and the commercial use of that knowledge.

E.4 The Exceptional-Character Limit

The case law and commentary treat the higher level of protection under Article 5(3) as an exceptional case relative to the average-consumer standard. This is the doctrinal reason why the DFA's broadened formulation is significant: it potentially converts an exceptional protective standard into a more general fairness obligation, which is precisely the textual choice the Path A mapping must track. The undue-influence route, by contrast, is not an exceptional standard in the same sense; it is an application of the general prohibition on aggressive practices.

